            "author": {
              "__typename": "User",
              "login": "DavinZhang"
            },
            "body": "Independently reproduced this on current `main` and confirmed the patch is correct.\n\nRepro without any GPU: `_build_scheduled_request_metrics()` never touches `self`, so it can be driven directly with a duck-typed decode batch. With `seq_lens_cpu=None` it raises `TypeError: 'NoneType' object is not iterable` at `metrics_reporter.py:285`; with this patch it returns `num_decode_requests=2, sum_decode_kv_tokens=21`, matching the `seq_lens_cpu=[8, 13]` control case. `merge_batch()` sets `seq_lens_cpu = None` whenever either side lacks the CPU mirror (`schedule_batch.py:3224-3226`), so this is a legitimate batch state, and the fallback to `req.seqlen` is exactly what `_decode_total_seq_lens()` already does — no extra device sync.\n\nTwo suggestions so this can land:\n\n1. **It needs a rebase.** Every check here is red because of the repo-wide maintenance gate (\"A major update has landed on `main`. All PRs must rebase onto the latest `main` before CI will run.\"), so no suite ever ran your change. That maintenance issue is closed now and this branch is ~2.7k commits behind `main`, so a rebase should finally get you a real CI run.\n2. **It has no regression test.** An eight-line guard with no test is easy to lose in a later refactor. `test/registered/unit/observability/test_forward_pass_metrics.py` already has all the fakes needed — this case fails before the patch and passes after it, and the whole `test/registered/unit/observability/` directory stays green (122 → 123 passed):\n\n```python\ndef test_emit_decode_batch_without_seq_lens_cpu(self):\n    \"\"\"A decode batch may legitimately carry no CPU mirror of seq_lens --\n    merge_batch() sets seq_lens_cpu to None whenever either side lacks it --\n    and iterating that None took the scheduler down. Decode KV stats must fall\n    back to per-request seqlen, the way _decode_total_seq_lens() already does.\n    \"\"\"\n    decode_a = _FakeReq(8, output_len=2)\n    decode_b = _FakeReq(13, output_len=1)\n    batch = self._make_batch(reqs=[decode_a, decode_b], decoding_reqs=[], seq_lens_cpu=None)\n\n    with patch(\n        \"sglang.srt.managers.scheduler_components.metrics_reporter.time.monotonic\",\n        return_value=101.0,\n    ):\n        self.reporter._emit_forward_pass_metrics(batch)\n\n    scheduled = self.scheduler._fpm_publisher.metrics[0].scheduled_requests\n    self.assertEqual(scheduled.num_decode_requests, 2)\n    self.assertEqual(scheduled.sum_decode_kv_tokens, decode_a.seqlen + decode_b.seqlen)\n```\n\nFeel free to take it as-is.\n",
            "createdAt": "2026-08-14T19:20:49Z",
            "databaseId": 5297261855,
            "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T19:20:49Z",
            "url": "https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/pull/31319#issuecomment-5297261855"
          },
          {
            "author": {
              "__typename": "User",
              "login": "Frank-whw"
            },
            "body": "Hi, we encountered the same scheduler crash (`TypeError: 'NoneType' object is not iterable` in `_build_scheduled_request_metrics`) with Forward Pass Metrics enabled in a different speculative-decoding deployment.\r\n\r\nI noticed that #31319 is now stale and does not include the regression test suggested in the review.\r\n\r\nWould a fresh, focused PR against current `main` be welcome? I can add the `seq_lens_cpu=None` decode-batch regression test alongside the existing fallback fix, and reference both #31309 and #31319.\r\n\r\nHappy to follow your preference.",
